import CTASection from '../../../components/CTASection.astro';
import { MANUFACTURER, TRADE_TERMS, RELATIONSHIP } from '../../../data/site';

const title = 'Battery Export Documentation | Dingwei Battery';
const description = 'The complete battery export document set — invoice, packing list, bill of lading, certificate of origin, test reports and the dangerous-goods declaration.';
const articleTitle = 'Battery export documentation';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'How many core documents does a battery export need?', a: '5 at minimum — commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading, certificate of origin and a dangerous-goods declaration — plus test reports and an MSDS where the destination or carrier requires them.' },
  { q: 'Which document do battery exporters most often miss?', a: 'The dangerous-goods declaration, which must be signed by a certified person. Skipping it is a fine, a delayed container or a refused shipment, because the battery is Class 8, not general cargo.' },
  { q: 'What does a certificate of origin prove?', a: 'Where the goods were manufactured — it is 1 of the 5 core documents and it unlocks preferential duty rates, which is why many destination customs authorities demand it by many destination customs authorities, so it is a compliance document as much as a formality.' },
  { q: 'When is a CCA or capacity test report required?', a: 'When the destination market or the buyer contract demands it — most markets want proof the battery meets its labelled specification (e.g. 72 Ah, 490 CCA), so the report travels with the shipment as evidence.' },
  { q: 'How do Incoterms change the document responsibility?', a: 'They decide who arranges and pays for each document and carriage leg across the 3 Incoterms we offer — under FOB the buyer controls freight and clearance; under CIF the seller arranges insurance and freight, with risk transferring at the origin port rather than the destination.' }
];
---
<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} articleTitle={articleTitle} articleSection="Logistics" dateModified="2026-09-10" faqs={faqs}>
  <section class="section container prose">
    <span class="tag">Knowledge · Logistics</span>
    <h1>Battery export documentation</h1>
    <p class="byline">Published by Dingwei Battery · Last reviewed 2026-09-10 · Sources: IMO IMDG Code, export logistics practice, Incoterms 2020</p>
    <p class="lead">A battery container moves on documents, not just on a ship. The paperwork set is what keeps a Class 8 load flowing through customs instead of sitting in a bond racking up demurrage. This page lists each document, what it proves, and the one most exporters forget.</p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> A lead-acid battery export needs at least five documents — commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading, certificate of origin and a dangerous-goods declaration — plus a CCA/capacity test report and MSDS where the destination or carrier requires them. The dangerous-goods declaration is the one amateurs miss, and it must be signed by a certified person because the battery ships as Class 8 corrosive.
    </div>

    <h2>The five-document baseline</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>What it proves</th><th>Who issues it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Commercial invoice</td><td>Value, terms, buyer and seller</td><td>Exporter</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Packing list</td><td>Carton counts, weights, dimensions per pallet</td><td>Exporter</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bill of lading</td><td>Receipt and contract of carriage</td><td>Carrier</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Certificate of origin</td><td>Where the goods were manufactured</td><td>Chamber of commerce</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dangerous-goods declaration</td><td>UN number, class and packing, signed</td><td>Certified shipper</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>The dangerous-goods declaration, in detail</h2>
    <p>This is the document that separates a professional export from an amateur one. Because lead-acid batteries are Class 8 corrosive under the [IMDG Code](https://www.imo.org/), the shipper must declare the UN number (UN 2794 or UN 2800), the proper shipping name, the class and the packing — and the declaration must be signed by a person certified in dangerous-goods regulations, under the rules set by the [UN Model Regulations](https://unece.org/transport/dangerous-goods). A battery shipped as "general merchandise" is a misdeclaration, and it is the most common hazmat violation in the industry. The full classification logic is in the <a href="/knowledge/lead-acid-battery-dangerous-goods/">dangerous-goods classification</a> page.</p>

    <h2>Supporting documents that travel with the load</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>CCA / capacity test report</strong> — proof the battery meets its labelled specification; requested by many destinations and embedded in most OEM contracts.</li>
      <li><strong>MSDS (material safety data sheet)</strong> — the safety data for the electrolyte and the battery; required by carriers and customs in most markets.</li>
      <li><strong>Test-report / conformity certificates</strong> — CE for Europe, SASO/GSO for the Gulf, or destination-specific conformity, where the market demands them.</li>
      <li><strong>Insurance certificate</strong> — evidence of cargo cover when the buyer requires it, especially under CIF.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The destination-specific requirements are mapped market by market in the <a href="/knowledge/battery-import-regulations/">import regulations</a> page.</p>

    <h2>How Incoterms divide the work</h2>
    <p>The Incoterm decides who arranges and pays for each document and each carriage leg. Dingwei Battery ships on {TRADE_TERMS.incoterms}:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>FOB</strong> (Free On Board) — the seller delivers to the port and clears export; the buyer takes risk and freight from the port onward.</li>
      <li><strong>CFR</strong> (Cost and Freight) — the seller also pays freight to the destination port, but risk still transfers at the origin port.</li>
      <li><strong>CIF</strong> (Cost, Insurance and Freight) — the seller adds insurance to the destination port; the buyer handles import clearance.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The practical point: under FOB the buyer owns the clearance documents and the freight; under CIF the seller arranges more of it. Choose the term that puts the documentation in the hands of whoever has the local relationships to clear it.</p>

    <h2>What slows a battery shipment down</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A missing or unsigned dangerous-goods declaration</strong> — the single most common hold.</li>
      <li><strong>A certificate of origin that does not match the invoice</strong> — discrepancies between documents trigger customs queries.</li>
      <li><strong>Test reports for a different model than the shipment</strong> — the report must match the actual batteries on the pallet.</li>
      <li><strong>Weights that do not reconcile</strong> — the packing list, invoice and bill of lading must agree to the kilogram.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The three discrepancies that hold a shipment</h2>
    <p>Most battery customs delays are not missing documents — they are documents that disagree with each other. Three mismatches cause the bulk of holds:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Invoice vs packing list weights</strong> — a kilogram difference between the two triggers a query. Reconcile to the kilo before dispatch.</li>
      <li><strong>Test report vs actual model</strong> — a CCA/capacity report for a different model than the batteries on the pallet invalidates the compliance claim.</li>
      <li><strong>Certificate of origin vs invoice</strong> — a mismatch between the origin country and the shipper's details raises a customs flag.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The rule is mechanical: one shipment, one consistent document set. Every number on the invoice must match the packing list and the bill of lading, and every test report must match the physical batteries. A 20-minute reconciliation before dispatch costs nothing; the same reconciliation at the destination border costs demurrage and a bond.</p>

    <h2>Who signs what</h2>
    <p>Documentation is not just paper — it is accountability, and each document carries a different signer:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Exporter</strong> — commercial invoice, packing list.</li>
      <li><strong>Carrier</strong> — bill of lading.</li>
      <li><strong>Chamber of commerce</strong> — certificate of origin.</li>
      <li><strong>Certified dangerous-goods person</strong> — the dangerous-goods declaration.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The dangerous-goods declaration is the one that requires a trained and certified signer, which is why it is also the one that separates a professional export from an amateur one. The <a href="/knowledge/battery-shipping-warranty/">shipping and warranty</a> page ties this document set to the packaging that protects the load.</p>

    <h2>Where this fits</h2>
    <p>{RELATIONSHIP} {MANUFACTURER.name} ships from {MANUFACTURER.exportPorts} on {TRADE_TERMS.incoterms}. The documentation page sits inside the logistics cluster:</p>
